The High Water Mark (HWM) is the highest point a segment has ever used. 
Oracle scans blocks only up to the HWM during a Full Table Scan. 
Blocks below the HWM may contain data or be empty after deletes, while blocks above the HWM have never been formatted or used.

Block 1  ███████
Block 2  ███████
Block 3  ███████
Block 4  ███████
Block 5  ███████

HWM
        ↓
Block 5

DELETE FROM employees
WHERE department_id = 10;

Block 1  ███████
Block 2  Empty
Block 3  ███
Block 4  Empty
Block 5  █████

HWM
        ↓
Block 5


but if we have:

Block 1
Block 2
Block 3
Block 4
Block 5

HWM = 5

TRUNCATE TABLE employees;

Block 1

HWM
 ↓
Block 1


SO:
| DELETE                                | TRUNCATE                                       |
| ------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------- |
| DML                                   | DDL                                            |
| Deletes rows one by one               | Removes all rows by deallocating table data    |
| Can use `WHERE`                       | No `WHERE` clause                              |
| Generates more Undo/Redo              | Generates much less Undo/Redo                  |
| **High Water Mark remains unchanged** | **High Water Mark is reset**                   |
| May cause fragmentation               | Does not leave fragmentation                   |
| May require `SHRINK SPACE` later      | No shrink is typically needed after truncation |


A Full Table Scan reads all blocks below the High Water Mark, including empty blocks created after DELETE operations. 
It does not scan blocks above the High Water Mark because they have never been allocated to the segment.





